WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.851
Good prompts make AI-assisted review more
precise.

2
00:00:05.931 --> 00:00:12.740
Instead of asking whether a file is
secure, ask a focused question:

3
00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:17.198
identify external inputs in this handler,

4
00:00:17.278 --> 00:00:24.696
check whether this authorization decision
protects object ownership,

5
00:00:24.776 --> 00:00:29.883
explain how this parser handles malformed
input,

6
00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:36.287
or list assumptions required for this
remediation to be safe.

7
00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:44.879
Small sections of code with clear context
usually produce better output than broad

8
00:00:44.959 --> 00:00:48.242
prompts over an entire project.

9
00:00:48.322 --> 00:00:56.591
A strong review prompt provides the
purpose of the code, the relevant framework,

10
00:00:56.671 --> 00:01:01.414
the trust boundary, and the specific
concern.

11
00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:08.669
It can ask the AI to separate confirmed
observations from hypotheses,

12
00:01:08.749 --> 00:01:14.342
cite the exact code behavior that supports
each claim,

13
00:01:14.422 --> 00:01:19.773
and state what additional evidence would
be needed.

14
00:01:19.853 --> 00:01:26.176
This forces the output into a structure
the reviewer can test.

15
00:01:26.256 --> 00:01:34.647
It also helps prevent vague advice that
sounds reasonable but cannot be tied to the

16
00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.186
application.

17
00:01:36.266 --> 00:01:40.766
Prompting is also part of the review
record.

18
00:01:40.846 --> 00:01:48.142
Capture important assumptions, decisions,
and rejected hypotheses.

19
00:01:48.222 --> 00:01:56.491
If the AI suggests a risk that is not
valid because of middleware, configuration,

20
00:01:56.571 --> 00:02:01.557
or a business rule, document why it was
rejected.

21
00:02:01.637 --> 00:02:09.541
If it identifies a plausible issue,
document the evidence that confirmed it.

22
00:02:09.621 --> 00:02:17.282
Avoid prompts that ask for unauthorized
testing, exploitation, evasion,

23
00:02:17.362 --> 00:02:20.159
or out-of-scope behavior.

24
00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:27.657
The review should remain defensive,
authorized, and evidence-driven.

25
00:02:27.737 --> 00:02:35.641
A practical numeric review also asks how
the value will be used after parsing.

26
00:02:35.721 --> 00:02:43.382
A value that is acceptable as text may
still be unsafe as an allocation size,

27
00:02:43.462 --> 00:02:48.083
loop bound, timeout, offset, or array
index.

28
00:02:48.163 --> 00:02:56.311
Ask the AI to identify each downstream
use, then verify the constraints in code.

29
00:02:56.391 --> 00:03:03.687
The safest fix usually validates at the
boundary and preserves a typed,

30
00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:12.158
range-checked value for later operations
instead of reparsing the same string in

31
00:03:12.238 --> 00:03:13.940
several places.
